## 10. Pure template packing and block-assembler concurrency

`authority/template.rs` already produces an immutable candidate receipt and
deterministic Pending proposal rank plus Proposed causal/conditional ordering.
It does not yet implement the old `TxSelector` cycles/bytes/CPFP packer. The
production cutover must provide one pure packer over that receipt with:

- exact cycles and serialized-bytes limits;
- ancestor-inclusive CPFP scoring and deterministic tie-breaking;
- parent-before-child inclusion;
- conditional reader-before-spender ordering only within the selected set;
- the existing bound of 4,000 consecutive candidate failures;
- bounded cycle/SCC handling and no mutation of authority state;
- the same implementation for production templates and internal `PackageTxs`.

The block assembler captures the UAK template receipt, paired snapshot, and
candidate-uncle source version, then constructs outside the authority lock.
Preserve the established optimistic concurrency model:

- full update and reset are mutually ordered at publication;
- full/reset construction may overlap partial uncle/proposal/transaction work;
- partial updates use optimistic source versions and may be skipped;
- full update retains publication priority;
- candidate uncles remain a separate bounded source, not a transaction owner;
- do not serialize full/reset/uncle/partial through one actor or one long-held
  lock.

Uncle filtering remains a template-derived liveness rule. It must exclude a
candidate uncle that conflicts with proposals selected from the current
authority receipt, so a detached block cannot indefinitely suppress recovered
proposal IDs.

## 11. Runtime topology and shutdown

The post-cutover task set is exactly:

- one ordered resolver;
- N parallel verification workers;
- one Ready driver;
- one bounded maintenance driver;
- one sole effect publisher;
- the existing verification-cache worker;
- the block-assembler loop with preserved optimistic concurrency;
- one ordered reorg/chain-administration handler;
- one message dispatcher.

Every state-changing task calls `AuthorityRuntime`; no task owns a secondary
queue or map. `Notify` values are lossy hints only; authoritative levels are
rechecked after subscribing and before waiting. No lock is held across await,
blocking I/O, callback, database access, VM verification, full graph traversal,
or retired payload destruction.

Shutdown order is:

1. stop external ingress and chain/admin producers;
2. cancel resolver, verification, Ready, and maintenance producers;
3. settle or stale every checked-out capability and join those workers;
4. close effect production;
5. drain and join the effect publisher;
6. capture one coherent persistence receipt and write it;
7. stop remaining derived services.

A worker task structural fault triggers controlled service shutdown. Legal
input, stale work, journal Full, endpoint timeout, peer behavior, or resource
pressure are typed outcomes and must not trigger service-wide fail-stop.

